WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:06.410
Hello, everyone.

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:07.850
My name is David Maguire.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:10.450
I am a data
scientist at dv01.

00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.150
We are a fintech startup
and we provide analytics

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:17.290
for investors in fixed income.

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:20.770
So something that I've heard
a few times over my career

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:23.170
is that R is bad for production.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:26.150
Sometimes engineers say,
oh, this looks great.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.870
But let's put it in another
language for production.

00:00:28.870 --> 00:00:30.430
They say R is ad hoc.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:31.960
It's for analysis.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.840
There's no package management.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.870
But today, I want to dispel
some of those notions

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.300
and I want to show you that
R is great for production.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:46.360
So first I'd like
to talk a little bit

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.410
about what I work on.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:52.150
So I work on Tape Cracker,
which is a web application that

00:00:52.150 --> 00:00:53.330
has a few components.

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:56.890
There's a JavaScript front
end, there's a Scala backend,

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:00.820
and then we have a machine
learning microservice

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.010
in R, which provides
predictions that

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:06.350
are used by the two other
components of the application.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.320
And this application is
used live by customers.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.300
They can get on at any time.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:13.190
They expect their
results immediately.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:17.170
So there's, there's very high
requirements for availability

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:18.480
and dependability.

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:22.360
Our sales team demos
this app live to clients

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.810
to prospective clients.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.580
So it has to work every time.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:28.580
If there is a failure we could
lose a prospective client.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.570
So this machine
learning microservice

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:34.880
has to be very
robustly engineered

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:40.520
to satisfy what our
customers expect of us.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:44.480
So I'm going to tell you, how
can you raise an R microservice

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:47.650
to thrive in the wild?

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:50.360
So here we have
our R microservice.

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:52.820
This cute little giraffe.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.320
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.830
is we're going to put our R
microservice through school.

00:01:57.830 --> 00:02:00.260
We're going to teach it what
to do once we release it out

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.000
into the wild.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.520
The end result of that is
that once we release it

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:08.000
into the wild, it can interact
with other microservices,

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.180
other applications written in
different languages seamlessly

00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:13.650
and just have a fun time.

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.840
So let's talk a little
bit more concretely.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:18.300
What is an R microservice?

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:23.210
So it's basically R code
behind plumber and Docker.

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.460
So we take our R code, our
machine learning model

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:28.650
and we put it behind
a plumber API.

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:31.830
This allows any
other application

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:33.960
to hit our machine
learning model

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.660
and it only has to provide
the expected inputs

00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:37.780
and it gets an output.

00:02:37.780 --> 00:02:40.920
So any other service doesn't
need to know anything

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:43.370
about the R language.

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:48.000
And then we wrap around
that plumber API.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.970
We put it in a Docker package.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:52.130
This makes our API portable.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:56.540
So once it's in a Docker
package or a Docker image,

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.110
we can deploy it into the cloud
on a variety of destinations.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:10.070
So a little bit more concretely
in our circumstance at dv01,

00:03:10.070 --> 00:03:13.110
the microservice while we
this is our web application.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:15.050
This is what it looks like.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.180
It provides a lot of
information that investors

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.850
need to make decisions.

00:03:19.850 --> 00:03:25.220
And we have a JavaScript front
end, we have a Scala backend,

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:28.910
and then behind that even we
have a machine data engineering

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:33.050
pipeline in Scala and
we have our R machine

00:03:33.050 --> 00:03:35.460
learning microservice
sits on top of that.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:38.000
And is able to provide
results to each

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.910
of the components
in that pipeline.

00:03:41.910 --> 00:03:46.020
So this is a great setup
for a data scientist

00:03:46.020 --> 00:03:50.070
because I can focus on
what I'm good at, which

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:53.050
is building models and
building data science projects.

00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:54.660
And then the software
engineers that I

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:58.380
work with, they can build
the end to end system.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.640
So very convenient.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.170
So in pursuit of building
robust R deployments,

00:04:06.170 --> 00:04:08.820
I want to introduce
three DevOps principles.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:10.850
The first is
continuous integration,

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.750
and that's where we build
and test our R packages.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:17.690
The second is
continuous deployment.

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:20.300
This is where we take
our tested packages

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.850
and release them into
production systems.

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:25.880
And then the third is
cluster computing,

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:31.010
which is a common destination
for applications

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:33.780
in production in the cloud.

00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.960
So let's start with
continuous integration.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:40.530
This is a simple continuous
integration workflow.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.620
So we start at the top
left where a data scientist

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.130
is working in R in RStudio,
making updates to the model,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:48.760
making updates to the code.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:53.730
So whenever a data scientist
makes an update the code base,

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:58.270
they will push that to a version
tracking tool such as GitHub.

00:04:58.270 --> 00:05:01.050
At that point, the
application will

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:04.000
be built from the source code.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:06.570
And then we're going to run
automated tests on that.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:08.440
And this happens automatically.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.340
Once I push all that
happens and then

00:05:11.340 --> 00:05:13.810
I'll get a report
back. If the test fail,

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:16.270
I know I have to go
back and fix something.

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:19.620
If they test pass,
I can then progress.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:23.160
I can merge my changes
into the master branch

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.330
and then we will
have an application

00:05:27.330 --> 00:05:31.000
that is ready for deployment.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.830
So let's look a
little bit closer

00:05:32.830 --> 00:05:37.760
into this first part
of the CI pipeline.

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:40.760
So this in the CI build step.

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:43.670
Basically, we have a data
scientist is working in RStudio

00:05:43.670 --> 00:05:46.820
writing R code and we
are going to Git push

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:50.120
and that's going to trigger
GitHub Actions to kick off

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.420
the continuous
integration pipeline.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.020
There's many options
for CI tools.

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.520
We use GitHub
Actions at dv01,

00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:02.630
and then the GitHub Actions is
going to build a Docker image

00:06:02.630 --> 00:06:05.540
based on the updated code.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:08.060
And what's important
here is that I

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:10.020
am pushing my code frequently.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:13.010
That means that I can get
instant feedback on what

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.160
each piece of code
that I edit and so I

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:17.670
know where changes happen.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:20.900
If I push 500 lines of code
at once and something fails,

00:06:20.900 --> 00:06:22.490
it's going to take
me a long time

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:23.880
to figure out what goes wrong.

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:26.120
But if I push a couple
lines at a time,

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:28.075
I can isolate the
problems very easily.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:33.440
So now let's look
at this next step.

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:36.600
We're running the
automated tests.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.500
So within continuous
integration tests,

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:43.920
we have three broad categories
of tests, sort of there's

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.170
this pyramid structure,
the base are unit tests.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:50.460
These are the most numerous
tests that we have.

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:51.190
Above those,

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:53.190
we have integration
tests which are still

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:55.440
a good amount of those,
but less than unit tests.

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:59.940
And finally, there are end
to end tests. And the

00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:05.340
testthat package is very useful
for writing these type of tests.

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:08.220
So starting at unit
tests, this is the base

00:07:08.220 --> 00:07:10.680
of our testing pyramid.

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.720
And the goal here is
to test functions,

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:16.290
individual lines
of code and really

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.340
look at each component
of your R package.

00:07:18.340 --> 00:07:21.630
So here I have a trivial
example of a unit test

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:24.570
where I, I define
this add 20 function

00:07:24.570 --> 00:07:28.410
and then I use testthat to
assert that if I add 20 to 10,

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.180
it should equal 30.

00:07:30.180 --> 00:07:32.610
And I define this
and this is going

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:36.010
to run every time I push
updates to my package.

00:07:36.010 --> 00:07:39.540
So if I were to make any
changes to this function that

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.000
would break it, I would
get immediate feedback.

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.830
And these type of tests are

00:07:45.830 --> 00:07:49.490
something that you should
have a lot of. For example,

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.730
it's a really good idea
to create unit tests

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:55.580
for your features, to make
sure that all the features that

00:07:55.580 --> 00:08:00.080
go into your models have
tests and/or never break

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:02.930
or never give unexpected input.

00:08:02.930 --> 00:08:06.680
If you're imputing missing
values at prediction time,

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:09.890
that would be a good
thing to unit test.

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:13.100
You can also test
like data structures,

00:08:13.100 --> 00:08:17.240
make sure that
functions return data

00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:19.790
frames of expected structures
and things like that.

00:08:19.790 --> 00:08:22.100
So writing a lot
of these unit tests

00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:25.040
allows you to make sure
that your code doesn't

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:30.200
break in unexpected ways and
it helps you identify failures.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:32.730
So the next level
is integration tests.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:34.880
So for integration
tests, the goal

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.549
is to make sure that
our R microservice can

00:08:37.549 --> 00:08:39.980
interact with other services
that it will need to.

00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:44.340
So we may be interacting
with databases or APIs.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:47.000
So we're going to write
tests and make sure

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:52.760
that our R container can interact
with every service it needs.

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:54.860
And finally, there
are end to end tests,

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.470
and this is where we're looking
at the whole microservices

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.630
application as a whole.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:03.260
So this is in our case, is
looking at our whole web app.

00:09:03.260 --> 00:09:06.680
It doesn't really tell us
much about our R microservice,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:11.130
but it's necessary to have a
holistic view of everything.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.420
Where we actually get useful
input about our R package

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:18.110
are the two previous
categories of tests.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:20.510
Now when we're writing
tests, we want to write them

00:09:20.510 --> 00:09:22.490
at the right time.

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.230
If you start writing
tests too early,

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.270
you can waste a lot
of time, but they save

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:27.450
a lot of time in production.

00:09:27.450 --> 00:09:29.450
So if you start a
new project when

00:09:29.450 --> 00:09:33.980
you're in the exploratory phase
doing EDA, initial experiments,

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:35.522
if you start writing
a lot of tests,

00:09:35.522 --> 00:09:36.980
you're going to
slow yourself down.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:41.630
But as you progress, as you get
your feature set more concrete,

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:43.400
as you get closer
to production, you

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:46.070
should start introducing
first unit tests, later

00:09:46.070 --> 00:09:47.070
integration tests.

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.400
And once you're really
close to production

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.310
to releasing a
microservice, you're

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.980
going to want to make sure
that the end to end tests

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:56.995
are properly configured and.

00:09:59.950 --> 00:10:03.100
So next we're going to talk
about continuous deployment.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:06.040
So at the end of the continuous
integration pipeline,

00:10:06.040 --> 00:10:10.510
we have our container
that's ready for deployment.

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:12.850
And what is going
to happen here is

00:10:12.850 --> 00:10:15.320
that this pipeline is going
to take that container,

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:16.960
and it's going to
push it through

00:10:16.960 --> 00:10:19.750
our different environment.

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:23.380
Here at dv01e, we have a
staging release and production

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:24.250
environment.

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:27.730
There's many ways to do
continuous deployment,

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:29.520
but this is just one example.

00:10:29.520 --> 00:10:33.010
So once the container
is ready, it's

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:34.550
going to be pushed to staging.

00:10:34.550 --> 00:10:39.250
And in staging there will be
some end to end tests that run

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.220
and we'll just make sure that
the whole application looks

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:44.860
good as a whole, that
if that goes well,

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:46.880
it moves on to release.

00:10:46.880 --> 00:10:48.980
And then finally it'll
go to production.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:51.920
And this is important because

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.380
we have a customer
facing application

00:10:54.380 --> 00:10:59.040
and we have zero tolerance for
any failures or for downtime.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.908
So we really need
to make sure that we

00:11:00.908 --> 00:11:02.450
have a lot of steps
in place to catch

00:11:02.450 --> 00:11:05.680
any errors that might come up.

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.790
And each of these
environments, the staging,

00:11:07.790 --> 00:11:10.030
the release ,and production
is a full replica

00:11:10.030 --> 00:11:11.620
of the production environment.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:15.490
It has our web application
as our R microservice,

00:11:15.490 --> 00:11:17.650
as well as all
the other services

00:11:17.650 --> 00:11:19.280
that make up the application.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:25.250
So let's talk a little bit
about the last concept, which

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:27.900
is cluster computing.

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:32.780
So in the cloud, a common way
to run jobs is in Kubernetes.

00:11:32.780 --> 00:11:35.540
So here we have our
production cluster

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:38.840
in which we have three replicas
of our machine learning

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:39.385
application.

00:11:39.385 --> 00:11:41.510
So it's important that we
don't just we're not just

00:11:41.510 --> 00:11:42.210
running it once.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:46.260
We're running it several
times that we have redundant.

00:11:46.260 --> 00:11:49.530
So when another microservice
comes along and says,

00:11:49.530 --> 00:11:52.920
hey R, give me
some predictions.

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:54.833
It's going to first
go to load balancer.

00:11:54.833 --> 00:11:56.250
The load balancer
is going to look

00:11:56.250 --> 00:11:59.940
at all the replicas of our R
machine learning microservice,

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:02.850
and it's going to route
it to one that is free

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:06.340
and that is error free.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:10.160
And then the R microservice
will respond back.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.390
And then the default
microservice application

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:15.255
can continue doing its work.

00:12:19.160 --> 00:12:21.590
And so what's important
here is I mentioned

00:12:21.590 --> 00:12:22.770
this is a ReplicaSet.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:25.890
So in kubernetes,
you have ReplicaSet,

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.760
which basically says that
for this application,

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.980
you're going to
have always going

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:35.660
to have three instances of
this application in this case.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:39.680
And if one of if one of
the replicas goes bad,

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:41.420
it will be torn
down and a new one

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:43.490
will be spun up immediately.

00:12:43.490 --> 00:12:46.310
If we're getting
high traffic, we

00:12:46.310 --> 00:12:53.730
can route new traffic to a free
replica of our microservice.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:54.980
So it's very important.

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.270
It gives us redundancy
and it allows us

00:12:57.270 --> 00:13:00.180
to handle high request loads.

00:13:00.180 --> 00:13:03.710
This also allows scaling.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:06.560
So in conclusion,
I just want to show

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:11.660
that R is great for production
with proper Ci and CD

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:12.480
practices.

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.340
Continuous integration of you
build and vet your R packages,

00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:22.280
continuous deployment help
you release updates seamlessly.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:23.770
Plumber

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:26.590
is an important component
for a microservice

00:13:26.590 --> 00:13:30.790
because it packages your
R code behind an API

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.580
where any service that
has no knowledge of R

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:36.530
can get results
from your R code.

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:40.010
And then Docker creates these
portable software packages

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:43.650
that you can run on your laptop,
you can run in the cloud.

00:13:43.650 --> 00:13:45.860
And then Kubernetes
is essentially

00:13:45.860 --> 00:13:48.170
